efine the pole-subtracted forward function $B(v)=A(2m^2+v)-P(2m^2+v)$. Assume $B$ is holomorphic on\n\n$$\\Omega_{\\nu_0}=\\mathbb C\\setminus\\bigl((-\\infty,-\\nu_0]\\cup[\\nu_0,\\infty)\\bigr),$$\n\nreal analytic there, meaning $B(\\overline v)=\\overline{B(v)}$, and has finite upper and lower boundary values on both cuts except at their endpoints. Here the two-particle gap is the open interval $(-\\nu_0,\\nu_0)$ in the $v$ variable; no massless pole or cut reaches the forward point $t=0$.\n\nRequire additionally that $B$ extends continuously from each open half-plane onto every compact segment of either real cut bank avoiding $\\pm\\nu_0$. This is an extra admissibility assumption on the supplied data, not a consequence of finite pointwise boundary values or a claim about every quantum field theory. \n\nAssume the exact high-energy condition $B(w)=o(|w|^2)$ uniformly as $|w|\\to\\infty$ in the closed upper half-plane, including upper cut-bank limits. In particular this holds on the large upper semicircles after any fixed bounded horizontal translation. For $\\nu\\ge\\nu_0$, define the absorptive density $\\rho(\\nu)=\\operatorname{Im}B(\\nu+i0)$. Require $\\rho$ to be nonnegative and Riemann integrable on every compact subinterval of $[\\nu_0,\\infty)$, and require $\\int_{\\nu_0}^{\\infty}\\rho(\\nu)\\nu^{-3}\\,d\\nu$ to converge as a finite improper Riemann integral. For every real $v$ with $|v|<\\nu_0$, require the integrals along both sides of the two cuts of $[B(x)-B(0)]/[x^2(x-v)]$, with boundary values taken from the upper and lower half-planes, to exist as finite improper Riemann limits. Require also that the small endpoint arcs at $\\pm\\nu_0$ for this integrand vanish in the contour limit. Since $P$ is real off its poles, $\\rho(\\nu)=\\operatorname{Im}A(2m^2+\\nu+i0)$ on the right cut.\n\nThe normalized forward Taylor coefficients are $c_n=B^{(2n)}(0)/(2n)!$ for integers $n\\ge1$. They have units $(\\text{mass})^{-4n}$; $\\nu_0$ has units $(\\text{mass})^2$. No sign is assigned here to a generic off-shell Lagrangian coefficient.",
